<%#
  One person in the People section of a search.

  Deliberately a pointer, not an expansion. Matching a customer means "here is
  a person", and the useful answer is the person once — not their forty tickets
  flooding the list and burying every message that actually contained the word
  you typed. customers/show is already the screen that lists all their tickets,
  so this row's whole job is to get you there.

  Locals: customer, counts ({"open" => 2, "closed" => 9} or nil)
%>
<% counts ||= {} %>
<% total = counts.values.sum %>

<%#
  turbo_frame: "_top" is load-bearing. This row renders inside the ticket_list
  frame, so without it Turbo scopes the visit to that frame, finds no matching
  frame in customers/show and replaces the row with "Content missing". Same rule
  as tickets/_ticket_row, and the same bug twice — any link added inside that
  frame needs it.
%>
<%= link_to customer_path(customer),
      data: {
        turbo_frame: "_top",
        row_id: "customer-#{customer.id}",
        shortcuts_target: "row",
        action: "click->shortcuts#enter"
      },
      class: "relative grid grid-cols-[1fr_132px] gap-6 py-[15px] pr-3 border-b border-rule hover:bg-hover hover:no-underline" do %>

  <%# A hollow ring, not a dot: this row is a person rather than a ticket, and
      the rail's filled/hollow vocabulary is about read state. %>
  <span class="absolute -left-[30px] top-5 w-[9px] h-[9px] rounded-full bg-canvas border border-faint"></span>

  <div class="min-w-0 flex items-baseline gap-2">
    <span class="font-medium tracking-[-0.01em]"><%= customer.display_name %></span>
    <span class="font-mono text-faint text-xs truncate"><%= customer.email %></span>
  </div>

  <div class="text-right text-sm text-soft">
    <%= total == 1 ? "1 ticket" : "#{total} tickets" %><%
      %><% if counts["open"].to_i.positive? %>, <%= counts["open"] %> open<% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
